The simplest smart pointer is **`Box<T>`**. `Box::new(value)` moves the value to the **heap** and leaves a pointer to it on the stack. When the box goes out of scope, both are freed. Boxes have no performance overhead beyond the heap allocation, and no extra abilities. Use them when:

1. you have a type whose size cannot be known at compile time, but need it in a context that requires a known size (recursive types, below);
2. you have a lot of data and want to transfer ownership **without copying** it;
3. you want a value of "some type that implements a trait" rather than one specific type: a **trait object**, `Box<dyn Trait>` (chapter 18).

